"The glass is modeled with its measured refractive index at each wavelength (the Sellmeier formula with the maker’s data for Schott N-BK7 crown glass and F2 flint glass). A thin lens of fixed shape has a focal length proportional to {#}/(n − {#}), so the colors that bend more focus nearer the lens.",
"Record reading stores the focal length of the lens or mirror at each of the seven colors, one row each (select the lens or mirror first if the bench has more than one). Export to worksheet sends the readings to the Data table below; Clear starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of the table.",
"Compare how a glass lens and a curved mirror focus white light, and see why every large modern telescope uses a mirror.",
"<strong>Lenses bend light.</strong> A lens focuses light by <strong>refraction</strong>: light slows down in glass and changes direction as it enters and leaves. How much it bends depends on the glass’s <strong>refractive index</strong>, and the index is a little different for each color. Glass bends violet light more than red light, which is how a prism makes a rainbow.",
"<strong>Chromatic aberration.</strong> Because violet bends more, a lens brings violet light to a focus a little closer than red light. No single place is in focus for every color, so a star seen through a simple lens has a colored fringe. This is called <strong>chromatic aberration</strong>. Early refracting telescopes suffered badly from it.",
"<strong>Mirrors reflect light.</strong> A curved mirror focuses light by <strong>reflection</strong>. The angle at which light bounces off a mirror does not depend on its color, so a mirror brings all colors to the same focus. Its focal length is half its radius of curvature, f = R/{#}, for every color.",
